{"name":"Electric Banana","hex":"#fbff00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electric","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, produced by, operated with, or utilising electricity; electrical.","Of or relating to an electronic version of a musical instrument that has an acoustic equivalent."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electricity; the electricity supply.","An electric powered version of something that was originally or is more commonly not electric.\n An electric car, locomotive, train etc.\n\nAn electric toothbrush.\n\nAn electric typewriter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electric"},{"term":"banana","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The elongated curved tropical fruit of a banana plant, which grows in bunches and has a creamy flesh and a smooth skin. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","In particular, the sweet, yellow fruit of the Cavendish banana cultivar, which may be eaten raw, as distinct from e.g. a plantain for cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Curved like a banana, especially of a ball in flight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banana"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Pretty Things","blocks":[{"text":"The Pretty Things were an English R&B and rock band formed in September 1963 in Sidcup, Kent, taking their name from Bo Diddley's 1955 song \"Pretty Thing\", and active in their first incarnation until 1971. They released five studio albums, including the debut The Pretty Things and S. F. Sorrow, four EPs and 15 UK singles, including the Top 20 UK Singles Chart \"Don't Bring Me Down\" and \"Honey I Need\". They reformed later in 1971 and continued through to 1976 issuing three more studio albums, and reformed once again from 1979 to 2020 releasing another five studio albums, the last of which was Bare as Bone, Bright as Blood."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/PrettyThings1965.jpg/330px-PrettyThings1965.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pretty_Things"},{"title":"Electricity","blocks":[{"text":"Electricity is the set of physical phenomena associated with the presence and motion of matter possessing an electric charge. Electricity is related to magnetism, both being part of the phenomenon of electromagnetism, as described by Maxwell's equations. Common phenomena are related to electricity, including lightning, static electricity, electric heating, electric discharges and many others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg/330px-London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity"},{"title":"Banana","blocks":[{"text":"A banana is an elongated, edible fruit that is botanically a berry produced by several kinds of large treelike herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa. In some countries, cooking bananas are called plantains, distinguishing them from dessert bananas. The fruit is variable in size, color and firmness, but is usually elongated and curved, with soft flesh rich in starch covered with a peel, which may have a variety of colors when ripe. It grows upward in clusters near the top of the plant. Almost all modern edible seedless (parthenocarp) cultivated bananas come from two wild species – Musa acuminata and Musa balbisiana, or their hybrids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Bananavarieties.jpg/330px-Bananavarieties.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banana"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Pretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_%28basgitarist%29%2C_Viv_Prince_%28drummer%29%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg/500px-Pretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_%28basgitarist%29%2C_Viv_Prince_%28drummer%29%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Pretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_%28basgitarist%29%2C_Viv_Prince_%28drummer%29%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg/1280px-Pretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_%28basgitarist%29%2C_Viv_Prince_%28drummer%29%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Pretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_%28basgitarist%29%2C_Viv_Prince_%28drummer%29%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg/500px-Pretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_%28basgitarist%29%2C_Viv_Prince_%28drummer%29%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Pretty Things in 1965","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APretty_Things_op_Schiphol._Vlnr_John_Stax_(basgitarist)%2C_Viv_Prince_(drummer)%2C_Bestanddeelnr_917-6655.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/The_Pretty_Things_1999_-_Phil_May_and_Skip_Alan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/The_Pretty_Things_1999_-_Phil_May_and_Skip_Alan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Phil May and Skip Alan in 1999","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Pretty_Things_1999_-_Phil_May_and_Skip_Alan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/The_Pretty_Things_1999.jpg/330px-The_Pretty_Things_1999.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/The_Pretty_Things_1999.jpg/330px-The_Pretty_Things_1999.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conten